      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "1. **Identify Each Angle's Symbol:** \n    - In the diagram, angle $P$ does not have a distinct label.\n2. **Determine Exterior Angles:**\n    - Draw extended lines from points $A$ and $B$ until they intersect.\n    - This setup forms several angles labeled and one forming the unknown.\n3. **Use Interior Angles Sum Property:**\n    - Sum of the measures of two angles on a line is $180^\\circ$.\n    - Sum of interior angles of a triangle equals $180^\\circ$.\n    - Sum of the angles around point $A$ equals $360^\\circ$.\n\n#### Step-by-Step Solution:\n\n### Looking at Two Labeled Interior Angles\n\n1. **Sum of Known Numbers:**\n   - We sum $128^\\circ$, $92^\\circ$, and $113^\\circ$.\n   \\[\n   128^\\circ + 92^\\circ + 113^\\circ = 333^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n2. **Remaining Angle:**\n   - Since the sum of the angles around $A$ is $360^\\circ$:\n   \\[\n   360^\\circ - 333^\\circ = 27^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n### Analyzing the Remaining Angle\n\n1. **Understanding Angle Types:**\n   - Each given angle is an interior angle in a triangle.\n   - Exterior angle of a triangle is equal to the sum of the two interior opposite angles.\n\n#### Looking at the Sum of the Other Numbers\n\n2. **Sum of the Unknown Interior Angles:**\n   - Sum of angles $BD$, $DE$, and $EA$ is:\n   \\[\n   111^\\circ + 113^\\circ + x = 360^\\circ\n   \\]\n   - Where $x$ denotes the unknown angle at point $P$.\n\n3. **Calculating the Missing Angle:**\n   - The sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral equals $360^\\circ$:\n   \\[\n   111^\\circ + 113^\\circ + x + P = 360^\\circ\n   \\]\n   - Simplifying gives:\n   \\[\n   2(P + 113^\\circ) = 360^\\circ - 224^\\circ\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2P + 224^\\circ = 360^\\circ\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2P = 136^\\circ\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P = 68^\\circ\n   \\]\n\nConclusion\n\\[\n\\boxed{68^\\circ}\n\\]",
