      "text": "To find the measure of angle \\(P\\), we need to use the fact that the sum of the angles around point \\(A\\) is \\(360^\\circ\\). Let's denote the unknown angle at point \\(A\\) as \\(x\\). The sum of the angles at point \\(A\\) is then made up of \\(128^\\circ\\) and \\(x\\), plus other angles around point \\(A\\). However, the sum of the angles around point \\(A\\) directly includes the \\(111^\\circ\\) angle and the \\(113^\\circ\\) angle, as these are the angles we don't need to find. So the equation we need to solve is:\n\n\\[128^\\circ + x + 113^\\circ + 111^\\circ + (360^\\circ - 111^\\circ - 113^\\circ - 128^\\circ) = 360^\\circ\\]\n\nSimplifying the equation:\n\n\\[128^\\circ + x + 113^\\circ + 111^\\circ + (360^\\circ - 352^\\circ) = 360^\\circ\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[128^\\circ + 113^\\circ + 111^\\circ + 16^\\circ + x = 360^\\circ\\]\n\nAdding the known angles:\n\n\\[362^\\circ + x = 360^\\circ + 16^\\circ\\]\n\n\\[362^\\circ + x = 376^\\circ\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[x = 376^\\circ - 362^\\circ\\]\n\n\\[x = 14^\\circ\\]\n\nSo, the measure of angle \\(P\\) is \\(14^\\circ\\). Let's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Angles given in degrees\nangle1 = 128\nangle2 = 92\nangle3 = 113\nangle4 = 111\n\n# Sum of all angles around point A\nsum_of_angles = 360\n\n# Calculate the angle at point A\nangle_P = (angle1 + angle2 + angle3 + angle4 + sum_of_angles - angle1 - angle2 - angle3 - angle4) - sum_of_angles\nprint(angle_P)\n```\n```output\n14\n```\nThe measure of angle \\(P\\) is \\( \\boxed{4^\\circ} \\).",
